Add an explicit, KEBAB-only code-signing policy for the iOS 16 bring-up.

The port has no AMFI kext and no TrustCache implementation, so nothing in the
kernel can tell an Apple platform binary from an arbitrary signed file:
CS_PLATFORM_BINARY is never set (that bit is AMFI's to set), every entitlement
query is denied, and the shared-cache SIP check rejects the port's root volume
because its image does not mark the cache files SF_RESTRICTED.  Each of those
is either a silent denial or a dead code path today, which makes the bring-up
hard to reason about.

Instead of sprinkling conditionals, add one policy module whose relaxations are
named bits of the "kebab_cs" boot argument:

  0x1  accept unsigned Mach-O files (already the config default because this
       kernel has neither SECURE_KERNEL nor CONFIG_ENFORCE_SIGNED_CODE; the
       switch pins it explicitly)
  0x2  classify a signed binary as a platform binary when its cdhash is in
       Apple's static trust cache, which is read from the IPSW component that
       BuildManifest calls "StaticTrustCache" (Firmware/<OS dmg>.trustcache,
       IM4P "trst", trust_cache_module v2, 2760 entries for 20H392)
  0x4  let the built-in AMFI provider answer entitlement queries with success
       instead of KERN_DENIED
  0x8  accept a root-owned dyld shared cache on this process' unsealed root
       volume without requiring SF_RESTRICTED

"kebab_cs=15" is the development preset.  With no boot argument nothing is
enabled, and the whole file is compiled only for KEBAB.

Deliberately untouched: structural validation of the signature blob, the code
directory and page hashes, CS_HARD/CS_KILL, CS_REQUIRE_LV/CS_RUNTIME, W^X and
the exec permission checks.  xnu never verified the CMS signature itself, so
that is not lost here; what is given up (trust-cache data on a writable root,
blanket entitlements) is written down in docs/BRING-UP-STATE.md section 12.
